            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Mandarin for sensible (of a child), then the Mandarin for to run the household."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: 父母",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "父母 — fùmǔ — \"parents; one's mother and father\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "wǒ gēn fùmǔ zhù le èrshí nián — I lived with my parents for twenty years."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The new character, in pieces: 母 (mǔ) — five strokes: a frame of two, one down the left that turns right along the bottom and one along the top that turns down the right side and hooks; a dot inside the top half; a long bar across; a dot inside the bottom half. The next lesson puts it in your hand."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "父母 (fùmǔ) is built from 父 'a father' and 母 (mǔ) 'a mother', both inherited from Middle Chinese, bjuX and muwX (Guangyun, in Baxter's transcription), which glosses the second 'a mother' and, after an older book, reads its two dots as a picture of a nursing mother's breasts."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
